// @run
// @log 1
// @log s
// @log 1
// @log 5
// Regression: a clause-2 site found by auditing `goal-scoreboard.py --sites` rather than by a
// filed row — `compiler/typecheck.vl`'s "an inferred union return through an un-annotated
// parameter is not yet supported by codegen; annotate the return type", one of the 22
// capability literals no corpus cell reaches.
//
// `function f(c) { if c { return 1 }  "s" }` has a union return the checker infers perfectly
// well. Passing its result to an UN-ANNOTATED parameter refused with `monomorphize:
// unsupported argument type`. Writing `: i32 | string` on `f` makes the identical program
// run, which is what says the type was known all along and only the pin channel could not
// see it.
//
// `monoArgTyName`'s call-argument rung read `acfn.fnRet >= 0` — an ANNOTATED return only.
// The checker's own record of an inferred one is `inferRetNameOf`, the same registry
// `inferRetBearsNull` reads for the void fixpoint, and routing it through `monoAnnPinName`
// keeps the pin to spellings that ladder already claims (a union NAME is its `isUName` rung)
// rather than whatever the inference happens to render.
//
// BOTH ARMS ARE PRINTED. A pin that named the wrong member would still compile and would
// print the wrong one of these two.
function f(c: boolean) {
  if c { return 1 }
  "s"
}

function id(x) { x }

print(id(f(true)))
print(id(f(false)))

// The annotated twin, which always ran and must keep running.
function g(c: boolean): i32 | string {
  if c { return 1 }
  "s"
}
print(id(g(true)))

// And a plain scalar through the same un-annotated parameter.
print(id(5))
